{
  "0": "Embedding representation: apply parameter-free LayerNorm to nonpadding input item embeddings immediately before the GRU, leaving candidate embeddings and scoring unchanged. Stabilizing recurrent input scale may reduce sensitivity to item embedding norms and improve validation HR@10. Keep embedding width, GRU topology, history/readout, loss and optimizer fixed. Apply across all four datasets, training one model per category; mirror normalization in the predictor writer.",
  "1": "Network architecture: add one jointly learned scalar carry gate per GRU layer: h_t=(1-alpha)*h_previous+alpha*h_GRU, with alpha=sigmoid(a), initialized to 0.9. This conservative recurrent update may retain useful interests through noisy interactions with negligible parameter overhead. Preserve padding behavior, features, embeddings, hidden widths, scoring/readout, loss and optimizer. Train one model per category across all four datasets and update the predictor writer.",
  "2": "Interest aggregation: replace the current readout with 0.8 times the last valid GRU state plus 0.2 times an exponentially weighted mean of valid recurrent states, using a five-interaction half-life and excluding padding. Recent-state pooling may recover interests lost by the final update while retaining strong recency. Preserve history selection, embeddings, GRU topology, scoring, loss and optimizer. Use one model per category across all four datasets and mirror readout in the predictor writer.",
  "3": "Training strategy: halve the existing learning rate throughout training, preserving the optimizer type and all other settings. Smaller updates may reduce the validation oscillation visible in Electronics and improve generalization without increasing per-epoch computation. Keep scheduler structure, batch size, dropout, weight decay, early stopping, epoch cap 200, seeds, architecture, readout and loss fixed. Train one model per category across all four datasets and retain single-checkpoint prediction."
}
